WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.212
A SELECT statement can do more than return stored
columns It can

00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:09.912
also rename output and calculate values In this
example full_name AS

00:00:09.938 --> 00:00:13.762
patient gives the full_name column a temporary label
of patient in the

00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:19.450
result The stored column in the table is still named
full_name The

00:00:19.475 --> 00:00:23.912
alias changes only what you see in this query output
The expression

00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:27.462
age greater than or equal to sixty-five creates a
new Boolean result

00:00:27.488 --> 00:00:34.050
called is_senior PostgreSQL evaluates that
expression for each row If

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:38.825
the patient's age is sixty-five or higher the result
is true Otherwise

00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:44.225
it is false This is an important idea A SELECT query
can

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.788
derive useful information without changing the
stored data We do not need

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.150
to add an is_senior column to the table just to
answer this

00:00:52.162 --> 00:00:57.350
question We can calculate it at query time Run the
query and

00:00:57.375 --> 00:01:02.362
compare age with the is_senior result Look for the
boundary around age

00:01:02.388 --> 00:01:07.288
sixty-five Then notice that the output is still
ordered by age descending

00:01:08.175 --> 00:01:11.738
Aliases are helpful when an original column name is
technical or when

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:16.138
an expression needs a readable heading Expressions
are helpful when you want

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.512
to calculate or classify values as part of the
result Both make

00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:23.462
SQL output more useful for analysis and reporting
